  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 149 AM MDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nThe last two nights I used music to describe the weather of the day. \nSo, why not make it three days in a row. Lets step back another \ndecade today, to the 60s. And let us also change music genres again, \nthis time to country. And this has to with a pattern that you \nwould expect more during the middle of summer, not a week from \nthe start of autumn. The song is by the late, great Johnny Cash,\nRing Of Fire. Basically, a strong ridge sets up in the southern\nUnited States and moisture rides along the northern edge of it.\n\nThere continues to be an strong ridge over the southeastern and \nsouth central United States, bring more record high temperatures. \nFor us in Wyoming, we will be close to the top of the ridge, with an \nincreasing chance of showers. For today and most of tomorrow, the \nbulk of the moisture looks to remain to the south of Wyoming. Just \nenough moisture will creep northward late today and especially \ntonight to bring some showers to the southern third of the state. \nBut these still look fairly isolated and even here most locations \nshould not see one (less than a 1 out of 4 chance). Moisture will \ntry to creep further north on Wednesday but even on this day the \nbulk will remain south of the border with only isolated activity. As \nfor temperatures, today looks fairly close to normal. With southerly \nflow increasing Wednesday, temperatures look warmer with highs \nrising to around 80 in some of the warmer spots.\n\nChances of showers should increase beginning Wednesday night and \nespecially into Thursday and Friday. The ridge over the southeastern \nUnited States with retrograde toward the southern plains. At the \nsame time, low pressure over the Pacific Northwest will begin to get \npushed eastward. moving toward Idaho by Thursday night. This will \npush the plume of monsoonal moisture further north and west and into \nthe Cowboy State. Precipitable water values could be rather high. \nThe GFS has values approaching an inch, which is approaching 200 \npercent of normal Wednesday night and Thursday. This model does tend \nto overcook this somewhat, so reality may be closer to 150 percent \nof normal. Nevertheless, the chance of showers and storms will \nincrease. Model guidance continues to have disagreements in the \nplacement of the most numerous showers though. So, we have higher \nconfidence in Thursday and Friday being the two more active days, \nbut placement of the most numerous showers and precipitation amounts \nremains uncertain. Temperatures will remain somewhat above normal, \nalthough with more cloud cover a bit cooler than on Wednesday. And \nlike most monsoonal events, all areas will have a chance of a shower \nor storm, but not everyone will see one.\n\nThere is more uncertainty for the weekend. There is still a decent \nmodel spread on how fast the low will exit the area. Most guidance \nnow takes the low north of the area across Montana. With flow \nturning northwestward, Saturday looks like a cooler day across the \narea. How fast the low moves will determine the extent of any \nprecipitation though since it will be of the wrap around variety. \nMost guidance favors northern Wyoming given the position of the low, \nwith lesser chances further south. Sunday looks somewhat drier \nalthough a few areas may still see a few showers around. Ridging \nshould build back over the area to start next week, bring a return \nto dry and seasonable weather.",
